 \section{Discussion}
 \subsection{Interpretation and limits of reference-budget effects}
-The ca-GrQc result changes the interpretation of an apparent budget effect. At default PageRank tolerance, increasing the reference budget changes the restricted method's mean comparison from a gain to a loss. Tightening tolerance exchanges two pool members and removes that pattern, although increasing $W$ still changes selected sets. Thus, the default reversal is not evidence for a budget effect that is independent of ranking precision. The candidate cutoff makes numerical ranking differences relevant to seed eligibility. Pool agreement at $10^{-12}$ and $10^{-14}$ checks membership stability at the tested tolerances, without certifying an exact ranking. Losses persist on CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote, so this explanation does not account for every unfavorable PageRank comparison.
+The ca-GrQc result changes the interpretation of an apparent budget effect. At default PageRank tolerance, increasing the shared selection budget changes the restricted-minus-full mean difference from positive to negative. Both seed-selection procedures vary with $W$, so this contrast cannot be attributed solely to an improving full reference. Tightening tolerance exchanges two pool members and removes that pattern, although increasing $W$ still changes selected sets. Thus, the default reversal is not evidence for a budget effect that is independent of ranking precision. The candidate cutoff makes numerical ranking differences relevant to seed eligibility. Losses persist on CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote, so this explanation does not account for every unfavorable PageRank comparison.
 
